## What this loop is

A photograph goes in. RF-DETR reads keypoints out of it, ANNY is fitted to those
keypoints, the fitted rig is rendered, and the render is scored against the photograph.
The referee is a second opinion answering a question EditScore cannot: whether a body
could hold that pose at all.

Nothing in this notebook runs until you run it. Every cell that touches the card is a
subprocess into a pixi environment, so reading the notebook costs nothing.

## Two facts that will bite before anything else does

**RF-DETR gives 17 joints and ANNY's `coco.pth` gives 23.** The extra six are foot
points. Comparing the two arrays directly compares different things, so the subset is
taken explicitly and named, not sliced silently.

**`render_corpus.py` requires the 19,158-vertex makehuman topology** with
`remove_unattached_vertices=False`. The 13,718-vertex body submodel that `anny_rig.py`
builds for the corpus is a different width, and `coco.pth` is indexed against the first.
One direction fails loudly. The other does not, which is the dangerous one.

```python
import sys, json
from pathlib import Path

HARNESS = Path(r"C:\weftspun-keypoint\7-service\service-livebook\priv\python")
CORPUS = Path(r"C:\weftspun-keypoint\6-datasource\anny-render-corpus")
CONSENSUS = Path(r"C:\weftspun-keypoint\3-interactor\pose-consensus\python")
sys.path.insert(0, str(HARNESS))
sys.path.insert(0, str(CONSENSUS))

from weft_loop import run, pixi_run, plot, household, PreconditionFailed

COCO17 = ["nose", "left_eye", "right_eye", "left_ear", "right_ear",
          "left_shoulder", "right_shoulder", "left_elbow", "right_elbow",
          "left_wrist", "right_wrist", "left_hip", "right_hip",
          "left_knee", "right_knee", "left_ankle", "right_ankle"]


def to_17(names, array):
    """The explicit subset. A missing name is an error, not a shorter array."""
    index = {n: i for i, n in enumerate(names)}
    missing = [n for n in COCO17 if n not in index]
    if missing:
        raise KeyError(f"the render asset is missing {missing}")
    return array[[index[n] for n in COCO17]]
```

## Inputs

```python
PHOTO = r"C:\anny_test\hv_0.png"
WORK = Path(r"C:\weftspun-keypoint\.loop1")
WORK.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
```

`hv_0.png` is an ANNY render standing in for a photograph so that the path resolves. It is
not a photograph, and a render scored against itself measures the renderer rather than the
fit, so point `PHOTO` at a real image before reading anything into the number.

`hv_0.png` is an ANNY render standing in for a photograph so that the path resolves. It is
not a photograph, and a render scored against itself measures the renderer rather than the
fit, so point `PHOTO` at a real image before reading anything into the number.

`hv_0.png` is an ANNY render standing in for a photograph, so that the path resolves. It is
not a photograph, and a render scored against itself measures the renderer rather than the
fit, so point `PHOTO` at a real image before reading anything into the number.

Do not point `PHOTO` at `coco_person_commercial_val2017`. That set is the blinded
holdout: not inspected while developing, not used to pick a checkpoint, a threshold or a
stopping point, and not looked at to decide whether this loop is working. A holdout
consulted during development has been trained on by hand, just slowly.

## Detect

```python
def detect(path):
    result = pixi_run("corpus", [
        str(Path(r"C:\weftspun-keypoint\1-transport\rf-detr-mcp") / "test_keypoints.py"),
        str(path),
    ])
    return result.stdout
```

RF-DETR runs on CPU here by design, so this step does not compete with the renderer or
the scorer for the card.

## Fit, render, score

```python
def propose(i):
    render = WORK / f"fit_{i}.png"
    pixi_run("anny", [
        str(CORPUS / "render_view.py"),
        str(WORK / f"fit_{i}.npz"), str(render),
        "--index", str(i - 1), "--views", "8",
        "--variant", "llvm_ad_rgb", "--threads", "1",
    ])
    return str(render)


def score(path):
    out = WORK / (Path(path).stem + ".score.json")
    pixi_run("editscore", [
        str(HARNESS / "weft_score.py"),
        "--source", PHOTO, "--edited", str(path),
        "--instruction", "match the pose of the person in the source photograph",
        "--out", str(out), "--precision", "nf4",
    ])
    return json.loads(out.read_text())["overall"]
```

`--variant llvm_ad_rgb --threads 1` is the reproducible renderer. `metal_ad_rgb` is 60
times faster and is not bit-reproducible, so it produces nothing a later round can be
compared against.

The view index walks the `sphere_hammersley_sequence`. A front view picked by hand shows
error of five stacked soda cans along the travel axis against three and a half across it,
which is a measurement of the view rather than of the fit.

## The fit, which does exist

**A previous version of this notebook said no points-to-pose solver existed here. That was
wrong, and the correction is worth more than the claim was.**
`4-entities/anny-pose-retarget-work/fit_joints_to_anny.py` solves a full 104-bone pose from
21 three-dimensional joint positions, by Umeyama for the global placement and LBFGS with a
strong-Wolfe line search for the rotations. What was missing was only the front end: that
file takes 3D world positions and a detector gives 2D pixels.

`priv/python/loop1_fit.py` is that front end, built on the same two ideas. The forward is
already differentiable -- `KeypointsRegressor` is a per-vertex convex blend from vertices to
the named keypoints, and everything from pose parameters to those keypoints is torch -- so
composing a weak-perspective camera onto the end makes the loss a reprojection distance and
no inverse model is needed.

```python
import numpy as np

from loop1_fit import build_model, fit_2d, residuals_by_region, detect_keypoints
from anny.keypoints import KeypointsRegressor

model = build_model()
regressor = KeypointsRegressor.coco(model)
target2d, confidence = detect_keypoints(PHOTO)
fit = fit_2d(model, regressor, target2d, confidence)
print(f"median {float(np.median(fit['residual_px'])):.2f} px of {fit['stature_px']:.0f} px stature")
```

**Measured, by a known-answer round trip rather than by inspection.** Pose the rig, project
its own keypoints through a known camera, throw the pose away and fit it back:
`priv/python/test_loop1_fit.py` recovers a median of 1.56 px on a 590 px stature, which is
0.263% of stature, or 4.5 mm on a 1.7 m body -- about one pencil. Shuffled targets reach
111.8 px, so the round trip is measuring the solve rather than the rest pose.

**The blocker moved, and it is now smaller.** Nothing here carries `rfdetr`: rf-detr-mcp
documents a local virtual environment that is not in the checkout, and no pixi environment
declares it either, so `detect_keypoints` raises. The fit needs none of that. Seventeen
points from anywhere -- a projection of an authored pose, a hand annotation, another
detector -- go straight into `fit_2d`.

```python
fit_residuals = residuals_by_region(fit["residual_px"])
stature = fit["stature_px"]
```

Residual and stature are both in pixels, so the referee's fraction-of-stature thresholds
apply unchanged. The fit does not recover depth: two poses that project to the same pixels
are indistinguishable to this loss, and `pose-consensus/python/depth_term.py` is what
exists for that and is not wired in here.

## The referee, which is not the score

```python
from soma_referee import referee


def report(fit_residuals, stature):
    """What to do with a fit, once there is one.

    A function rather than loose statements, because loose statements would name two
    variables nothing in this notebook can produce, and a cell that cannot run should say
    so by taking its inputs rather than by failing at the first line.
    """
    verdict = referee(fit_residuals, stature)
    print(verdict.call)
    for region, pct in verdict.residual_pct.items():
        print(f"  {region:>10}  {pct:5.2f}% of stature  {verdict.household(region)}")
    return verdict
```

`FITS`, `IMPOSSIBLE`, `HANDS_UNTRUSTED` or `NOT_RUN`. A missing region is `NOT_RUN` and
never a pass, which is the rule the harness applies to a missing environment as well.

**Expect `NOT_RUN` here, and that is the correct answer rather than a fault.** The referee
judges five regions and this loop can supply two. Face and hands need what a 17-point
detector does not carry, so the honest verdict is that the fit was not refereed, not that
it passed. Closing that gap is what a wholebody head is for; until then this cell reports
which regions were unavailable rather than averaging over the ones that were.

## Run it

```python
history = run(propose, score, control=PHOTO, rounds=3)
print(history.table())
plot(history)
```

## Provenance

```python
record = {
    "loop": "1-keypoints-to-anny",
    "photo": PHOTO,
    "detector": {"model": "RFDETRKeypointPreview", "joints": 17},
    "rig": {"topology": "makehuman", "vertices": 19158, "keypoint_asset_points": 23},
    "renderer": {"engine": "mitsuba", "variant": "llvm_ad_rgb", "threads": 1,
                 "cameras": "sphere_hammersley_sequence"},
    "fit": {"solver": "loop1_fit.fit_2d", "camera": "weak perspective",
            "round_trip_median_px": 1.56, "round_trip_pct_of_stature": 0.263},
    "baseline": history.baseline,
    "rounds": [r.__dict__ for r in history.rounds],
}
(WORK / "provenance.json").write_text(json.dumps(record, indent=2, default=str), encoding="utf-8")
print("wrote", WORK / "provenance.json")
```
